# Line endings are normalised to LF in the repo AND in the working tree.
#
# This exists because of a real failure, not as hygiene. Cortana is edited on a
# Windows box and runs on a Linux one, and Python's write_text() on Windows
# silently translates \n to \r\n. install.sh was committed with CRLF and, on the
# Linux box, every line failed:
#
#     install.sh: line 4: $'\r': command not found
#     E: Invalid operation update            (from `apt update\r`)
#     E: Unable to locate package git        (from `git\r`, last on its line)
#
# What made it expensive is that it passed every check on the dev box: `bash -n`
# under Git Bash tolerates CRLF, so the syntax check said OK while the script
# could not run at all on the machine it was written for. A guard that passes in
# the wrong environment is not evidence - see CLAUDE.md.
#
# eol=lf (not just text=auto) is deliberate: text=auto alone would still hand a
# Windows checkout CRLF in the working tree, and the file that gets scp'd,
# pasted, or read by a subagent is the working-tree one.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Never touch these - normalisation would corrupt them.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.ico    binary
*.woff   binary
*.woff2  binary
*.ttf    binary
*.apk    binary
*.jks    binary
*.p12    binary
*.onnx   binary
*.wav    binary
*.mp3    binary
*.zip    binary
*.jar    binary
